<%# Crawl fallback for product pages: the copy a fetcher reads otherwise exists only inside the
    Inertia payload, so without JavaScript it receives an empty shell (gumroad-private#4521).
    noscript keeps this out of the rendered UI, where the React page already shows the same
    product, so the text here duplicates the page rather than cloaking it.
    Name, seller and price are plain escaped ERB output — never html_safe — because noscript is
    a raw-text element and an unescaped closing tag in any of them would end it early.
    The description is stored HTML, so it arrives already reduced to escaped plain text; the
    gsub is the one remaining guarantee that it cannot close the element. %>
<noscript>
  <article>
    <h1><%= @seo_product_fallback[:name] %></h1>
    <% if @seo_product_fallback[:seller].present? %>
      <p>by <%= @seo_product_fallback[:seller] %></p>
    <% end %>
    <% if @seo_product_fallback[:price].present? %>
      <p><%= @seo_product_fallback[:price] %></p>
    <% end %>
    <%= @seo_product_fallback[:description].to_s.gsub(%r{</noscript}i, "&lt;/noscript").html_safe %>
  </article>
</noscript>
